event: response.function_call_arguments.done
data: {"type":"response.function_call_arguments.done","sequence_number":720,"item_id":"fc_0bfe6e1fa8f24ccf016adb3c10273c8191b4feee83de70411f","output_index":1,"arguments":"{\"artifact_json\":\"{\\\"base_candidate\\\":11,\\\"patches\\\":[{\\\"op\\\":\\\"add\\\",\\\"path\\\":\\\"/graph/inferences/-\\\",\\\"value\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"R003\\\",\\\"inputs\\\":[\\\"N001\\\",\\\"H003\\\",\\\"M001\\\",\\\"M004\\\",\\\"M005\\\",\\\"M006\\\"],\\\"output\\\":\\\"N002\\\",\\\"definitions\\\":[\\\"D001\\\",\\\"D002\\\"],\\\"rules\\\":[\\\"direct\\\",\\\"forall_introduction\\\"],\\\"locals\\\":[{\\\"symbol\\\":\\\"r\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"positive real\\\",\\\"scope\\\":\\\"R003\\\",\\\"start\\\":\\\"S001\\\",\\\"end\\\":\\\"S001\\\",\\\"kind\\\":\\\"arbitrary\\\",\\\"declaration\\\":\\\"Fix arbitrary r>0.\\\",\\\"from_inputs\\\":[],\\\"from_local\\\":[],\\\"objects\\\":[]}],\\\"argument\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"S001\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"Let J=[r/2,3r/2] and take compact C from H003. M005 gives compact C×J. The continuous e_t is bounded there by K and vanishes outside C. The function b=K·1_C is integrable since C is compact and has finite Lebesgue measure. For F(τ)(x)=e(x,τ) and F′(τ)(x)=e_t(x,τ), every F(τ) is strongly measurable by continuity, F(r) integrable by N001, and F′(r) strongly measurable. Smoothness gives the pointwise derivative in τ, and |F′(τ)(x)|≤b(x) for τ∈J. M006 therefore applies and gives E differentiable at r with derivative ∫e_t(x,r)dx. This proves N002 for arbitrary r.\\\",\\\"uses\\\":[\\\"N001\\\",\\\"H003\\\",\\\"M001\\\",\\\"M004\\\",\\\"M005\\\",\\\"M006\\\"],\\\"uses_local\\\":[],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"r\\\",\\\"e\\\",\\\"E\\\",\\\"u\\\"],\\\"produces\\\":[],\\\"introduces\\\":[],\\\"discharges\\\":[]}],\\\"applications\\\":[{\\\"input\\\":\\\"M006\\\",\\\"at\\\":\\\"S001\\\",\\\"bindings\\\":\\\"α=ℝⁿ,μ=Lebesgue,𝕜=E=ℝ,x₀=r,s=(r/2,3r/2),F(τ)(x)=e(x,τ),F′(τ)(x)=e_t(x,τ),bound=K1_C.\\\",\\\"hypothesis_checks\\\":[\\\"r lies in the open neighborhood interval.\\\",\\\"Each F(τ) is continuous, hence a.e. strongly measurable.\\\",\\\"F(r) integrable by N001.\\\",\\\"F′(r) is continuous and strongly measurable.\\\",\\\"Compactness of C×J and continuity bound e_t on C; it vanishes outside C, yielding domination.\\\",\\\"K1_C is integrable because C has finite Lebesgue measure.\\\",\\\"Smoothness proves the pointwise derivative identity.\\\"]},{\\\"input\\\":\\\"M005\\\",\\\"at\\\":\\\"S001\\\",\\\"bindings\\\":\\\"X=ℝⁿ,Y=ℝ,s=C,t=J.\\\",\\\"hypothesis_checks\\\":[\\\"Both factors are Euclidean topological spaces.\\\",\\\"C compact by H003.\\\",\\\"J is a compact closed bounded interval.\\\"]},{\\\"input\\\":\\\"M004\\\",\\\"at\\\":\\\"S001\\\",\\\"bindings\\\":\\\"α=C×J,β=ℝ,s=C×J,f=e_t.\\\",\\\"hypothesis_checks\\\":[\\\"C×J compact by M005.\\\",\\\"Euclidean spaces have uniform structures.\\\",\\\"e_t continuous by smoothness.\\\"]}]}}]}\"}"}

event: response.output_item.done
