=K1_C.\\\",\\\"hypothesis_checks\\\":[\\\"r lies in the open neighborhood interval.\\\",\\\"Each F(τ) is continuous, hence a.e. strongly measurable.\\\",\\\"F(r) integrable by N001.\\\",\\\"F′(r) is continuous and strongly measurable.\\\",\\\"Compactness of C×J and continuity bound e_t on C; it vanishes outside C, yielding domination.\\\",\\\"K1_C is integrable because C has finite Lebesgue measure.\\\",\\\"Smoothness proves the pointwise derivative identity.\\\"]},{\\\"input\\\":\\\"M005\\\",\\\"at\\\":\\\"S001\\\",\\\"bindings\\\":\\\"X=ℝⁿ,Y=ℝ,s=C,t=J.\\\",\\\"hypothesis_checks\\\":[\\\"Both factors are Euclidean topological spaces.\\\",\\\"C compact by H003.\\\",\\\"J is a compact closed bounded interval.\\\"]},{\\\"input\\\":\\\"M004\\\",\\\"at\\\":\\\"S001\\\",\\\"bindings\\\":\\\"α=C×J,β=ℝ,s=C×J,f=e_t.\\\",\\\"hypothesis_checks\\\":[\\\"C×J compact by M005.\\\",\\\"Euclidean spaces have uniform structures.\\\",\\\"e_t continuous by smoothness.\\\"]}]}}]}\"}","status":"completed"}}

